"The question \"Have you not seen?\" at the beginning of the surah addresses the Prophet (peace be upon him) and expresses seeing with the heart's eye, that is, certain knowledge. The Incident of the Elephant was seen by many people living in that era, a universally known event that took place even while figures such as the famous poet Labid were alive. As narrated from Aisha, two of those who led the elephant were seen begging in Mecca, blind and crippled. Because this event was a sign portending the birth of the Prophet, he was specifically addressed.\n\nAllah's asking \"how He dealt\" is to draw attention to the quality of the action. For the astonishing aspect of the incident is how it occurred. It was not an ordinary destruction, but took place in an extraordinary manner. With this question, Allah reminds people of the wonder of His treatment of the companions of the elephant and warns them against heedlessness. Theologians call this \"the aspect of proof\" (wajh al-dalil); that is, those who are heedless of the subtleties of the qualities cannot fully grasp the truth.\n\nThe companions of the elephant were the Abyssinian army under the command of Abraha, who had invaded Yemen. Relying on Mahmud, a large elephant, they came to destroy the Kaaba, but were unsuccessful and were devastated. This event is known among the Arabs as the \"Year of the Elephant,\" and the Prophet was born in this year—according to reports, fifty days after the incident. When calculated according to the Hijri calendar, it is stated that this event corresponds to the 880s CE.\n\nAllah rendered their plots futile. He ruined their secret plans, termed \"kayd,\" by abandoning them to straying and error. Their aim was to demolish the Kaaba and replace it with the church of al-Qullays. Yet they were devastated before they could even drive their elephants into Mecca. Then Allah sent upon them the ababil birds. Ababil means flocks of birds coming in successive groups and droves. These birds were strange birds never seen before; some were large, some small, in black, green, and white colors.\n\nThese birds threw stones of baked clay (sijjil) at the companions of the elephant. Sijjil refers to stones of mud hardened like tiles, derived from the Persian words \"sang\" (stone) and \"gil\" (mud). According to reports, these stones were the size of lentils, chickpeas, or hazelnuts. It is related that each bird carried three stones—one in its beak and two in its claws—and that whichever person was struck, the stone pierced straight through their body. Thus, the army was torn to shreds like eaten straw. The expression \"asf ma'kul\" likens them to fresh crop leaves eaten and scattered by animals, describing how they were destroyed.\n\nThis event is a clear proof of Allah's power. As Fakhr al-Din al-Razi also noted, this incident took place so recently and before so many witnesses that had it been a fabrication, the Meccan polytheists would have immediately denied it. Yet none of them denied it; on the contrary, many poets recounted this incident in their poems. Therefore, rather than attempting to explain this story through natural causes, one must accept it as an extraordinary act of Allah and take heed.\n\nEven if extraordinary events recorded in history do not conform to normal laws, science does not deny them; rather, it records and examines them, however rare they may be. The Quran's account of the Incident of the Elephant is just like this: this incident is an exceptional event demonstrating Allah's power and has been transmitted through mutawatir (successively reported, definitive knowledge) chains. The Prophet (peace be upon him) lived in the aftermath of this event and grew up hearing of it from his surroundings. For this reason, he was addressed with: \"Have you not seen how your Lord dealt with the companions of the elephant?\"\n\nThe Incident of the Elephant begins during the Abyssinian rule in Yemen, when Abraha set out to destroy the Kaaba. Abraha had built a grand church in Sana'a and intended to redirect the pilgrimage of the Arabs to it. Enraged when the Arabs desecrated the church, Abraha marched upon Mecca with a vast army and elephants. Realizing they could not resist, the Quraysh withdrew to the mountains. Abd al-Muttalib went to Abraha, demanded the return of his camels, and stated that the protector of the Kaaba was Allah.\n\nAs Abraha was preparing to enter Mecca, the great elephant among them named Mahmud refused to move forward. At that very moment, Allah Almighty sent flocks of birds from the sea. Each bird carried stones like chickpeas and lentils in its beak and claws. These stones rained down upon the Abyssinian army, and whomever was struck was destroyed. The army was routed, and the fleeing soldiers trampled one another on the roads. Abraha was also wounded, his limbs falling off piece by piece, and he died.\n\nIn some reports, it is said that following this event, smallpox and measles were seen in Arabia for the first time. However, these reports are weak (da'if) and isolated. In a narration transmitted from 'Ikrimah, it is stated that smallpox broke out in the people struck by the stones thrown by the birds. But this too does not rely on a reliable chain of transmission. In the face of the clear account of the Quran and the widely accepted reports, such narrations carry little weight.\n\nCertain orientalists and contemporary writers have attempted to portray this incident as an ordinary epidemic of disease. However, Elmalılı criticizes such interpretations. In particular, he sternly rejects Abduh's attempt to obscure the miraculous aspect of the incident by elevating a weak narration to the rank of mutawatir. He emphasizes that the Incident of the Elephant was a manifest divine punishment and that the birds' stones were an integral part of it. Attempting to explain the event through natural causes diminishes its extraordinary nature and its instructive value.\n\nWhen commenting on this surah, Abduh first states: The surah reminds us of the greatness of Allah's power. A people, relying on their elephants, wished to harm the servants of Allah, and Allah destroyed them, thwarting their plans. Understanding this much is sufficient for drawing an admonition.\n\nAbduh then states that it is permissible to delve into details regarding the Incident of the Elephant. For this incident is a well-known and mutawatir report. Indeed, the Arabs used to date events according to the Year of the Elephant. For this reason, detailed elaboration is permissible.\n\nHowever, Elmalılı points out that there is an error in this detailed elaboration by Abduh. Under the classification of 'mutawatir,' Abduh relates only this: An Abyssinian commander came with a large army to demolish the Kaaba, reached the vicinity of Mecca, and sent an envoy. Yet Abduh says, 'On the second day, smallpox and measles spread in the Abyssinian army,' which is not mutawatir.\n\nElmalılı refutes this claim of Abduh. The narrations of 'Ikrimah and Ya'qub ibn 'Utbah are weak, and in reality, their destruction by the birds throwing stones is what is mutawatir. Abduh distorted these narrations to fit his own view, ignored the expressions 'birds' and 'stones,' and reduced the incident to a disease.\n\nAbduh goes even further, asserting that the birds were flies or disease-bearing creatures, and the stones were mud clinging to their feet. Elmalılı emphasizes that this is contrary to the clear declaration of the Quran. The surah explicitly speaks of 'flocks of birds' and stones of 'sijjil' (baked clay). Interpreting these as flies and dust is distortion.\n\nElmalılı states that the correct course is to believe as conveyed by the Quran and authentic narrations. Explaining the incident as a normal epidemic of disease diminishes divine power. Allah does whatever He wills, and it is not right to confine extraordinary events within the limits of human reason.\n\nIn this section, Elmalılı addresses a controversial issue regarding the commentary of Surah al-Fil. He strongly rejects the view of certain commentators that the stones cast upon them by the birds were the agents of smallpox or measles. In his view, this interpretation contradicts both the wording and the spirit of the verse, because the verse explicitly mentions stones called 'sijjil' (hardened clay). There is neither a rational nor a textual proof to restrict these stones to an illness such as smallpox. Furthermore, such an interpretation limits Allah's power and imposes conditions on His act of creating as He wills.\n\nElmalılı specifically criticizes the interpretation of Abduh. Abduh interpreted the birds as invisible microbes and explained the stones as microbe-bearing stones. According to Elmalılı, this interpretation is weak from the standpoint of both traditional narration (riwayah) and analytical reasoning (dirayah). He states that during the period when the surah was revealed, the Arabs did not derive the meaning of microbes from these verses; rather, they understood the event as visible birds and stones. He maintains that this interpretation of Abduh distorts the plain meaning of the verse and weakens the message of the surah.\n\nElmalılı emphasizes that the most admonitory aspect of the Incident of the Elephant is that the army, coming to demolish the Ka'bah relying on its own might, was routed by Allah in an entirely unexpected manner. The verse, 'Did He not make their plan go astray?' constitutes the core of the event. The sending of birds and stones, on the other hand, are secondary verses that explain the nature of this divine intervention. In the face of such a clear event, the fact that even the polytheists attributed it to Allah shows that the event was no ordinary occurrence.\n\nHe cites a question posed by Fakhr al-Din al-Razi: 'Even though the disbelievers of Quraysh filled the Ka'bah with idols, why did Allah punish not them, but those who sought to demolish the Ka'bah?' Elmalılı also relays the answer given by al-Razi: 'Because filling the Ka'bah with idols is a violation of Allah's right, whereas demolishing it is a violation of the people's right. Worldly punishments are predominantly inflicted for violations committed against the rights of servants. The punishment for violating Allah's right, however, fundamentally belongs to the Hereafter.' Furthermore, the wisdom behind this event was that it served as a preparation for the birth and prophetic call of the Messenger of Allah.\n\nTo defend the historical reality of the Incident of the Elephant, Elmalılı provides examples from poems composed during that period. He notes that in the poems of poets such as Abdullah ibn al-Ziba'ra, Abu Qays Sayfi, and Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt, they recount the destruction of the army with birds and stones. These poems demonstrate that the event was acknowledged even by adversaries and transmitted through successive reporting (tawatur). In light of these proofs, Elmalılı opposes those who deny or allegorically interpret (ta'wil) the event, emphasizing that at the time the surah was revealed, no one contradicted these accounts.\n\nLastly, as Elmalılı continues his critique of Abduh's interpretation, he remarks that the fine ideas in his work lose value due to such weak interpretations. He also draws attention to the error of certain translators who render the verse as 'they threw microbe-bearing stones.' In his view, such interpretations are unacceptable in the face of the surah's explicit wording. He states that well-intentioned people of knowledge must exercise caution in this regard."